{"summary": "Fixed the ERR_PNPM_EXOTIC_SUBDEP error hit by `pnpm dlx create-pantoken-app`. Root cause: `@pantoken/core` declared the GitHub-only `@instructure/instructure-design-tokens` as a runtime `dependencies` entry, needed only by its internal `buildTokens()` transform, but platform packages (android/compose/flutter/rust/swift, aggregated by `@pantoken/pantoken`) depend on core too for unrelated helpers — making the exotic git dependency a transitive subdependency for any scaffolded app, which pnpm rejects.

Fix: split `@pantoken/core` into a main entry (clean, no exotic dep) plus a new `@pantoken/core/build` subpath entry (holding `buildTokens`), following the existing `plugin-kit` sandbox-split convention. Moved the exotic dependency to `devDependencies` (still resolves for in-repo builds). Updated the one real consumer (`formats/tokens/scripts/generate.ts` + its test) and doc-comment/README examples across `packages/core`, `formats/dtcg`, and the `plugins/pantoken/*` packages to import `buildTokens` from `@pantoken/core/build`.

Verified: `grep instructure-design-tokens packages/core/dist/*.mjs` only matches the new `build.mjs`; rebuilt all platform packages + `@pantoken/icon-font` and confirmed none of their dist bundles reference the exotic package; ran `packages/core/tests/build.test.ts` and `formats/tokens` tests (pre-existing unrelated test failures reproduced identically on unmodified main, confirmed not caused by this change). Recorded findings in repo memory."}
